Most guides to hiring a freelance SEO consultant read like a checklist written by someone who has never actually been the client. This one starts from the other direction. It covers what UK business owners have actually reported going wrong when they hired one, what that means for vetting and contracting a freelancer, and what a realistic first 90 days looks like. I do this work myself, so the deliverables section is what I'd expect to see from any freelancer I evaluated, not just what I'd put in a pitch.

## What a Freelance SEO Consultant Actually Does

A freelance SEO consultant works directly with you, rather than through an account manager who relays your questions to whoever is actually doing the work. That's the entire structural difference from an agency, and it's worth being specific about what it should produce.

**Strategy and diagnosis:**

- A technical audit that identifies crawl, indexing, and rendering problems, not a generic checklist
- Keyword research tied to search intent and commercial value, not just search volume
- A [topical map](/services/topical-maps/) defining what to build before any content is written
- Competitive analysis that explains why a competitor outranks you, not just that they do

**Implementation guidance:**

- Content briefs with semantic and structural specifications
- On-page recommendations for existing pages, not just new ones
- Internal linking that connects content on purpose
- Schema markup where it earns eligibility for rich results

**Measurement:**

- Search Console data tied to what changed and when, not a vanity ranking screenshot
- Reporting on a cadence you agreed in writing, delivered on that cadence

What a consultant should not do: guarantee positions, promise page one within a fixed number of weeks, or go quiet on methodology when you ask a direct question.

## What UK Business Owners Actually Report Going Wrong

Search "SEO consultant" on UK Business Forums and you find years of threads from business owners describing what they paid for versus what they got. Three patterns come up repeatedly, and they tell you more about what to check than any generic red-flag list does.

**Reporting promised in the contract, never delivered.** One owner [signed a six-month contract at £500 a month](https://www.ukbusinessforums.co.uk/threads/help-not-happy-with-seo-companys-work.315488/) that specified weekly invoices and reporting. Neither arrived. When they asked for evidence of the link-building work they were paying for, the contractor couldn't produce any. The contract itself had another client's name still on it, a detail that should have ended the conversation before it started. Forum advice was to terminate for breach rather than negotiate, since the missing weekly invoices were themselves a breach of the agreed terms.

**Work that actively damages rankings.** A different owner [paid roughly £3,500 over five months](https://www.ukbusinessforums.co.uk/threads/seo-company-below-performance.382667/) for on-site work, off-site work, and blog content. Some target keywords dropped rather than improved. The blog content was, in their words, sentences strung together with no real editorial judgement, and it damaged the site's existing HTML in the process. They went on to spend around £6,500 correcting it. The lesson from that thread isn't "get everything in writing" in the abstract, it's specific: keep dated records of exactly what was delivered and when, because that's what you need if it goes wrong.

**Not knowing how to compare a freelancer against an agency in the first place.** A [separate thread](https://www.ukbusinessforums.co.uk/threads/would-you-hire-a-freelance-seo-consultant.392837/) asked why business owners default to distrust of freelancers specifically. The replies were candid: plenty of agencies delegate to junior staff behind an account manager who has never touched the account's data, and plenty of freelancers are undifferentiated generalists chasing link-building as the whole of SEO. The advice that held up across responses was to check a candidate's own rankings, not just their client case studies, and to talk to a past client directly rather than reading a testimonial on their site.

None of this is unique to freelancers. Agencies produce the same failures at higher day rates. But a freelancer has nowhere to hide behind a brand, which cuts both ways: you get direct accountability, and you also get zero backstop if the one person you hired turns out to be the problem.

## Freelance or Agency

This isn't a universal better-or-worse call. It depends on budget, scope, and what you actually need managed.

A freelancer tends to suit a business with a monthly budget under roughly £3,000, a need for specialist depth in one area rather than broad coverage, and a preference for talking directly to the person doing the work. An agency tends to suit a business that needs SEO bundled with PPC, social, or design, needs 50 or more pages of content produced monthly, or has a procurement process that requires an established company rather than a sole trader.

Plenty of businesses run a hybrid: a freelance consultant for strategy and technical oversight, with an in-house or agency team executing. I work this way with a handful of clients, providing the topical map and content briefs while their own team writes and publishes. I've laid out the full comparison, including where each model breaks down, in [freelance consultant versus agency](/blog/seo-consultant-vs-agency/).

## What It Costs

Pricing surveys and freelancer rate calculators don't agree closely enough to quote a single number with confidence, so treat any figure here as a market range rather than a fixed price. Across the sources I checked for 2026, day rates for UK SEO freelancers cluster roughly between £350 and £700 depending on experience, with London adding a further 20 to 30 percent. Monthly retainers for an experienced independent consultant typically sit between £800 and £3,500, with senior specialists in competitive niches charging more.

What moves the number: how competitive your market is, whether the work is advisory-only or includes implementation, and whether the consultant specialises in your industry. A freelancer quoting comprehensive SEO at £200 a month is not spending meaningful time on your account, whatever the pitch says. I've broken pricing down by experience tier and scope in [how much a freelance SEO consultant costs](/blog/how-much-does-an-seo-consultant-charge-uk/).

## Red Flags Before You Sign

- **Guaranteed rankings.** No one controls Google's algorithm closely enough to promise a position. "Page one in 30 days" is either dishonest or describes a keyword with no commercial value.
- **No documented evidence of past work.** Ask for Search Console screenshots showing before and after, not a rank tracker graph, which can be manipulated, and not a case study with no verifiable data behind it.
- **A contract that doesn't match what's delivered.** If the agreement specifies weekly reporting, weekly reporting should show up. The forum thread above shows what happens when it doesn't: you have no record to point to when things go wrong.
- **Vague methodology.** "Best practices" is not a process. A competent consultant can describe how they research, prioritise, and measure, in specific terms.
- **Content with no editorial judgement behind it.** Thin, generic, keyword-stuffed writing is the single most common complaint in the forum threads above, and it's the easiest thing to check before you sign anything: ask to see content they've written or directed.
- **Long lock-in contracts.** A consultant confident in their own work doesn't need a 12-month minimum to keep you. Monthly or quarterly terms protect both sides.
- **Retained ownership of deliverables.** Everything produced, audits, topical maps, briefs, should belong to you when the engagement ends. A consultant who keeps the strategy documents is building dependency on purpose.

## Sole Trader or Limited Company: What It Means for Hiring

Most UK freelance SEO consultants operate as either a sole trader or through their own limited company, and it changes what you as the hiring business need to think about.

If they're a sole trader, IR35 doesn't apply. It's an off-payroll rule that only concerns work done through an intermediary company, so a sole trader relationship is closer to a straightforward supplier contract. You're not responsible for determining their employment status, but you should still be clear in the contract that they control how and when the work is done, since that's what keeps the relationship outside disguised employment territory if it were ever questioned.

If they operate through a limited company, IR35 (the off-payroll working rules) can apply. Since April 2026, more businesses that had previously counted as medium-sized now fall under the "small company" threshold. That shifts the obligation to issue a status determination back onto the contractor rather than you. If your business isn't small under the Companies Act definition, you may still need to assess status yourself. This is worth a five-minute conversation with your accountant before you sign anything substantial, not something to work out after HMRC asks.

Either way, a written contract matters regardless of structure: what's delivered, on what cadence, who owns the output, notice period, and what happens to access (Search Console, analytics, CMS logins) when the engagement ends. The UK Business Forums case above shows what happens without one: a contract that didn't even have the right client's name on it, and no leverage when the promised reporting never arrived.

## Questions to Ask Before You Sign

1. **What is your specific process?** Listen for a described sequence of steps, not buzzwords.
2. **Can I see Search Console data from a past client?** A testimonial isn't verifiable. A screenshot with real numbers is closer.
3. **What will I own at the end of this, and when do I get it?** Confirm audits, briefs, and strategy documents transfer to you.
4. **How will you report, and how often?** Get the cadence in writing, then check it's actually happening a month in.
5. **What's your experience in my sector?** Not essential, but it shortens the ramp-up.
6. **What happens if the results don't show up on the timeline we agreed?** Listen for an honest answer, not reassurance.
7. **Who else touches this work?** If they subcontract writing or technical fixes, you should know to whom.
8. **What's the notice period, and what do I keep if I leave?** This is the question the forum complaints above wish they'd asked first.
9. **Are you a sole trader or a limited company?** Relevant to the IR35 conversation above, and to who's liable if something goes wrong.
10. **Can I contact a past client directly?** Not a written testimonial, an actual conversation.

For the fuller evaluation framework behind these, see [how to choose an SEO consultant](/blog/how-to-choose-seo-consultant/).

## What the First 90 Days Should Look Like

**Month 1:** A technical and content audit, competitive analysis, and the start of a topical map. You should receive priority recommendations with a stated reason for the priority, not just a task list.

**Month 2:** Technical fixes on the issues found in month one, on-page work on the highest-priority pages, and content briefs for the gaps identified in the topical map.

**Month 3:** The first new content published against that map, early ranking movement on the pages that were touched, and a report that connects specific changes to specific Search Console movement.

Meaningful traffic growth generally takes four to six months of consistent work, not three. If a consultant is promising visible results inside the first month, ask what they mean by "results". It's rarely traffic. [How long SEO takes](/blog/how-long-does-seo-take/) covers the realistic range and why it varies by competition level.

